Template: homelab-drive/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host to bootstrap this app's roles/schema on:
 Used only once, at first install, to run homelab-bootstrap-app-role.

Template: homelab-drive/pgbouncer_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PgBouncer host to register this app's runtime role with:
 Used only once, at first install. Also written into config.yml's
 database.host, so the running service connects through the pooler.

Template: homelab-drive/api_base
Type: string
Default: http://localhost:3000
Description: Base URL where homelab-api is reachable:
 Written into config.yml's homelab_api.base_url — every login and
 token check this app does goes through here.

Template: homelab-drive/listen_port
Type: string
Default: 2501
Description: Port for homelab-drive to listen on:

Template: homelab-drive/advertise_host
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1
Description: This host's own address to advertise in the service registry:
 Written into config.yml's registry.host -- what homelab-api's gateway
 actually connects to when forwarding /api/v1/drive/* requests here.
 Leave as 127.0.0.1 only if this service runs on the SAME host as
 homelab-api itself; set it to this host's real reachable address
 (e.g. its data-plane IP) for any distributed deployment, or every
 gateway forward will try to reach homelab-api's own loopback instead
 of this service.

Template: homelab-drive/sso_base_url
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:2502
Description: Base URL where homelab-sso is reachable:
 Written into config.yml's sso.base_url. homelab-sso is the only place
 a user ever types a password -- this app redirects here for /login
 and /logout.
 .
 IMPORTANT once this app and homelab-sso are on different hosts: this
 is a BROWSER redirect, not a server-to-server call -- it must be an
 address the end user's own browser can actually reach (e.g.
 homelab-sso's own public homelab-webproxy vhost, https://login.
 example.com), never an internal-only data-plane IP/port. A raw
 internal address here produces a login redirect the browser can't
 connect to at all -- found live (2026-09-26) on this exact field,
 same mistake this project's own homelab-roundcube package already
 hit and documented for its own sso_base_url -- see that package's
 debian/homelab-roundcube.templates for the fuller writeup. See
 sso_internal_base_url below for this app's OWN server-to-server call.

Template: homelab-drive/sso_internal_base_url
Type: string
Default:
Description: Internal (server-to-server) base URL for homelab-sso (blank = same as the public URL above):
 Written into config.yml's sso.internal_base_url only -- the one OAuth
 call this app's own backend makes directly (exchanging a code for a
 token), never through the end user's browser. Leave blank on a
 single-host install (falls back to homelab-drive/sso_base_url,
 unchanged behavior). Set this explicitly, to an internal address like
 http://10.50.2.48:2502, once this app and homelab-sso are on separate
 hosts -- confirmed live (2026-09-26) that reusing the PUBLIC url for
 this call breaks once that's true, the exact same hairpin-routing
 failure already found for homelab-roundcube's own oauth_token_uri
 (this app's own backend calling out to its own fleet's public IP has
 to loop back through the edge proxy to reach a different internal
 host, which this environment's network doesn't support).

Template: homelab-drive/sso_redirect_uri
Type: string
Default: https://drive.test.mailmasker.org/oauth/callback
Description: This app's own OAuth callback URL:
 Must match exactly what's registered for the "drive" client in
 homelab-sso's own config (homelab-sso/drive_redirect_uri at that
 package's install time).

Template: homelab-drive/sso_client_secret
Type: password
Description: Shared secret for this app's "drive" OAuth client:
 Must match exactly what homelab-sso's own install printed for the
 "drive" client (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-sso to see it again, or
 rotate it). Unlike homelab-sso's own client secrets, there's no
 sensible default to auto-generate here -- this app is the consumer of
 a secret homelab-sso mints, not the minter.
